WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.365
Here is a puzzle from real work. An app gets a licence. The licence is real. It

2
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:10.071
has not expired. And the app still refuses it.

3
00:00:10.421 --> 00:00:12.195
Refuses it? Why?

4
00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:19.036
The error says: rejected, iat future. Today we find out what that means. Three

5
00:00:19.036 --> 00:00:23.779
parts. What iat is. The check. And why a clock causes it.

6
00:00:24.129 --> 00:00:29.933
First, what is a licence? Think of it as a signed note. It says who it is for.

7
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:32.016
Say, organisation forty two.

8
00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:38.190
It says when it was made. That field is called iat. It stands for issued at.

9
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.606
And it says when it stops working. That one is called exp. Expires.

10
00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:45.253
And the signature?

11
00:00:45.603 --> 00:00:51.260
The signature proves nobody changed the note. If you edit even one number, the

12
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.264
signature no longer matches. So the app can trust what the note says.

13
00:00:56.614 --> 00:01:02.346
Next. How does a computer store a time like ten o'clock? As a number. It counts

14
00:01:02.346 --> 00:01:02.927
seconds.

15
00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:07.245
To keep the numbers small, let's count seconds since midnight.

16
00:01:07.595 --> 00:01:13.249
One minute is sixty seconds. One hour is sixty times sixty. That is three

17
00:01:13.249 --> 00:01:15.495
thousand six hundred seconds.

18
00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:21.226
So ten o'clock is ten times three thousand six hundred. Thirty six thousand.

19
00:01:21.826 --> 00:01:24.102
Do real tokens count from midnight?

20
00:01:24.452 --> 00:01:30.607
No. Real ones count from the first of January, nineteen seventy. Same idea, just

21
00:01:30.607 --> 00:01:33.838
bigger numbers. Today, midnight is enough.

22
00:01:34.188 --> 00:01:39.839
Now the check. The app refuses the licence if iat is greater than now plus

23
00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:40.374
thirty.

24
00:01:40.724 --> 00:01:46.170
Now means the clock of the computer doing the check. Not the clock that made the

25
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:46.715
licence.

26
00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:48.435
Why plus thirty?

27
00:01:48.785 --> 00:01:54.651
Because no two clocks agree exactly. They drift by a few seconds. Thirty seconds

28
00:01:54.651 --> 00:01:58.390
is grace, so tiny differences don't break anything.

29
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:05.266
Example one. The licence was made at ten o'clock. So iat is thirty six thousand.

30
00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:10.833
The checking computer's clock is two minutes behind. It thinks it is nine fifty

31
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:14.862
eight. That is thirty five thousand eight hundred and eighty.

32
00:02:15.212 --> 00:02:20.601
Now plus thirty. Thirty five thousand eight hundred and eighty, plus thirty, is

33
00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:23.466
thirty five thousand nine hundred and ten.

34
00:02:23.816 --> 00:02:29.336
Is thirty six thousand greater than thirty five thousand nine hundred and ten?

35
00:02:29.336 --> 00:02:34.360
Yes. So the licence is refused. It looks like it comes from the future.

36
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.820
So the licence was fine. The clock was wrong.

37
00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:42.804
Exactly. That is called clock skew. Two clocks that disagree.

38
00:02:43.154 --> 00:02:49.390
Example two. Same licence, iat thirty six thousand. This time the clock is only

39
00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:55.073
two seconds behind. Nine fifty nine and fifty eight seconds. Thirty five

40
00:02:55.073 --> 00:02:58.152
thousand nine hundred and ninety eight.

41
00:02:58.502 --> 00:03:04.263
Plus thirty is thirty six thousand and twenty eight. Is thirty six thousand

42
00:03:04.263 --> 00:03:09.026
greater than that? No. Accepted. The grace window did its job.

43
00:03:09.626 --> 00:03:15.318
Your turn. The clock is thirty one seconds behind. Work out now, then now plus

44
00:03:15.318 --> 00:03:18.529
thirty, then decide. Pause here if you want.

45
00:03:21.529 --> 00:03:26.763
Now is thirty six thousand minus thirty one. Thirty five thousand nine hundred

46
00:03:26.763 --> 00:03:27.769
and sixty nine.

47
00:03:28.119 --> 00:03:32.035
Plus thirty is thirty five thousand nine hundred and ninety nine.

48
00:03:32.385 --> 00:03:35.371
Thirty six thousand is bigger. So refused?

49
00:03:35.721 --> 00:03:40.452
Refused. By one single second. That is how sharp the edge is.

50
00:03:41.052 --> 00:03:47.637
There is a second check. The app also refuses a licence if iat is greater than

51
00:03:47.637 --> 00:03:48.988
or equal to exp.

52
00:03:49.338 --> 00:03:51.628
Made after it expired? How?

53
00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:58.213
A real licence is always made before it expires. If not, someone edited it, or

54
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:03.089
the program that made it has a bug. No clock fix helps there.

55
00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:08.881
So how do you fix the first problem? Not by making the grace window bigger. A

56
00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.496
bigger window lets more bad notes in.

57
00:04:11.846 --> 00:04:18.213
You fix the clock. Computers can ask a time server for the exact time. That is

58
00:04:18.213 --> 00:04:24.743
called NTP. On Windows, it is Settings, Time, Sync now. On a phone, set date and

59
00:04:24.743 --> 00:04:26.294
time automatically.

60
00:04:26.644 --> 00:04:30.036
So when I see iat future, I check the clock first.

61
00:04:30.386 --> 00:04:33.577
Yes. The clock of the computer that refused it.

62
00:04:33.927 --> 00:04:39.605
Three lines to remember. One. iat is when the note was made, in seconds.

63
00:04:39.955 --> 00:04:45.675
Two. Refuse if iat is greater than now plus thirty. That means a clock is

64
00:04:45.675 --> 00:04:46.223
behind.

65
00:04:46.573 --> 00:04:53.416
Three. Refuse if iat is greater than or equal to exp. That means the note is

66
00:04:53.416 --> 00:04:54.046
broken.

67
00:04:54.396 --> 00:04:56.736
Got it. Clock first, then the note.
